    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Weeds tend to follow exponential growth.
                Assume that the number of weeds in a field at time <m>t</m>
                given in days is
                <md>
                    N(t) = 4^{0.2t}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many weeds are there in the field initially?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many weeds would we expect on day 18?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the parameter 0.2 was changed to 0.205, how many weeds
                    should we expect on day 18?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Notice we increased the parameter 0.2 by <m>0.25\%</m>.
                    By how much percent did the output change?
                    Would you consider this model sensitive to that parameter?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What if we go back to our original model, but instead change
                    the parameter 4 to 4.05.
                    This is a <m>1.25\%</m> increase of that parameter.
                    By how much percent did the output change?
                    Would you consider this model sensitive to that parameter?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If a storm at time 18 blew more seed into the field so that
                    the number of weeds went up by 100 instantaneously at that
                    time, and independently of the exponential growth model, how
                    would we adapt our model?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>